<exercisegroup xml:id="exercisegroup-col-row-null-space-test-el-span">
<title> Determining membership in a span </title>

<introduction><p>
	In each of the following,
	use column space to determine whether vector <m> \uvec{v} </m> is in <m> \Span S </m>.
	In those cases where it is,
	express <m> \uvec{v} </m> as a linear combination of the spanning set vectors.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>

		<m> S = \{ (1, 1, 0, 0), (5, -4, 3, -5), (6, -6, 7, -7) \} </m>;

		<m> \uvec{v} = (1, -3, 8, -3) </m>

	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Create a matrix <m> A </m> whose columns are the vectors in <m> S </m>:
		<md>
			A =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				1 \amp  5 \amp  6 \\
				1 \amp -4 \amp -6 \\
				0 \amp  3 \amp  7 \\
				0 \amp -5 \amp -7
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>.
		Then our question becomes whether <m> \uvec{v} </m> is in the column space of <m> A </m>,
		which happens precisely when the system <m> A \uvec{x} = \uvec{v} </m> is consistent.
		<md>
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
				1 \amp  5 \amp  6 \amp  1 \\
				1 \amp -4 \amp -6 \amp -3 \\
				0 \amp  3 \amp  7 \amp  8 \\
				0 \amp -5 \amp -7 \amp -3
			\end{abmatrix}
			\qquad \rowredarrow \qquad
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
				1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>
		Since the system is inconsistent,
		<m> \uvec{v} </m> is not in <m> \Span S </m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>

		<m> S = \{ (3, 2, 1, 0, 1), (4, 3, 1, 0, 3), (1, 1, -1, 0, 1) \} </m>;

		<m> \uvec{v} = (3,2,5,0,5) </m>

	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Create a matrix <m> A </m> whose columns are the vectors in <m> S </m>:
		<md>
			A =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				3 \amp 4 \amp  1 \\
				2 \amp 3 \amp  1 \\
				1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \\
				1 \amp 3 \amp  1
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>.
		Then our question becomes whether <m> \uvec{v} </m> is in the column space of <m> A </m>,
		which happens precisely when the system <m> A \uvec{x} = \uvec{v} </m> is consistent.
		<md>
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
				3 \amp 4 \amp  1 \amp 3 \\
				2 \amp 3 \amp  1 \amp 2 \\
				1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 5 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \amp 0 \\
				1 \amp 3 \amp  1 \amp 5
			\end{abmatrix}
			\qquad \rowredarrow \qquad
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
				1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -3 \\
				0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp  4 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -4 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp  0
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>
		Since the system is consistent,
		<m> \uvec{v} </m> is in <m> \Span S </m>,
		with
		<md>
			\uvec{v}
			= (-3) \begin{bmatrix}     3 \\ 2 \\  1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
			+   4  \begin{abmatrix}{r} 4 \\ 3 \\  1 \\ 0 \\ 3 \end{abmatrix}
			+ (-4) \begin{abmatrix}{r} 1 \\ 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{abmatrix}
		</md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>

		<m>
			S = \left\{
				\begin{bmatrix}       1 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp  0 \end{bmatrix} ,
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}  2 \amp 1 \\ 3 \amp -1 \end{abmatrix},
				\begin{bmatrix}       2 \amp 1 \\ 4 \amp  1 \end{bmatrix}
			\right\}
		</m>;

		<m> \uvec{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp 2 \\ 8 \amp 6 \end{bmatrix} </m>

	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Create a matrix <m> A </m> whose columns are the coordinate vectors of the vectors in <m> S </m>,
		relative to the standard basis for <m> \matrixring_{2 \times 2}(\R) </m>:
		<md>
			A =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				1 \amp  2 \amp 2 \\
				0 \amp  1 \amp 1 \\
				2 \amp  3 \amp 4 \\
				0 \amp -1 \amp 1
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>.
		Then our question becomes whether the coordinate vector for <m> \uvec{v} </m> is in the column space of <m> A </m>,
		which happens precisely when the system <m> A \uvec{x} = \rmatrixOf{\uvec{v}}{S} </m> is consistent.
		<md>
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
				1 \amp  2 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\
				0 \amp  1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
				2 \amp  3 \amp 4 \amp 8 \\
				0 \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp 6
			\end{abmatrix}
			\qquad \rowredarrow \qquad
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
				1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
				0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp  4 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp  0
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>
		Since the system is consistent,
		<m> \uvec{v} </m> is in <m> \Span S </m>,
		with
		<md>
			\uvec{v}
			= (-1) \begin{bmatrix}      1 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp  0 \end{bmatrix}
			+ (-2) \begin{abmatrix}{rr} 2 \amp 1 \\ 3 \amp -1 \end{abmatrix}
			+   4  \begin{bmatrix}      2 \amp 1 \\ 4 \amp  1 \end{bmatrix}
		</md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement>

		<p><m>
			S = \left\{
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}  2 \amp 0 \\ -1 \amp  0 \\  1 \amp  1 \end{abmatrix},
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}  0 \amp 1 \\  0 \amp -1 \\  0 \amp -1 \end{abmatrix},
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr} -1 \amp 1 \\  1 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp  0 \end{abmatrix},
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}  2 \amp 4 \\  1 \amp -3 \\  0 \amp  1 \end{abmatrix}
			\right\}
		</m></p>

		<p><m> \uvec{v} = \begin{abmatrix}{rr} 6 \amp 6 \\ 0 \amp -5 \\ 2 \amp 2 \end{abmatrix} </m></p>

	</statement>

	<solution><p>
		Create a matrix <m> A </m> whose columns are the coordinate vectors of the vectors in <m> S </m>,
		relative to the standard basis for <m> \matrixring_{3 \times 2}(\R) </m>:
		<md>
			A =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
				 2 \amp  0 \amp -1 \amp  2 \\
				 0 \amp  1 \amp  1 \amp  4 \\
				-1 \amp  0 \amp  1 \amp  1 \\
				 0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp -3 \\
				 1 \amp  0 \amp -1 \amp  0 \\
				 1 \amp -1 \amp  0 \amp  1
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>.
		Then our question becomes whether the coordinate vector for <m> \uvec{v} </m> is in the column space of <m> A </m>,
		which happens precisely when the system <m> A \uvec{x} = \rmatrixOf{\uvec{v}}{S} </m> is consistent.
		<md>
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr|r}
				 2 \amp  0 \amp -1 \amp  2 \amp  6 \\
				 0 \amp  1 \amp  1 \amp  4 \amp  6 \\
				-1 \amp  0 \amp  1 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
				 0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp -3 \amp -5 \\
				 1 \amp  0 \amp -1 \amp  0 \amp  2 \\
				 1 \amp -1 \amp  0 \amp  1 \amp  2
			\end{abmatrix}
			\qquad \rowredarrow \qquad
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr|r}
				1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>
		Since the system is inconsistent,
		<m> \uvec{v} </m> is not in <m> \Span S </m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>

		<md>\begin{split}
			S = \{
				\amp x^6 + x^5,
				x^6 + x^5 + x^2 - x + 1,
				x^6 - x^5 - x^4 + 3 x^3 + 3, \\
				\amp 2 x^6 + 2 x^5 - x^3 + x^2 + 1,
				x^6 - 2 x^5 + 2 x^3 - 3 x^2 + x + 1
			\}
		\end{split}</md>

		<m> \uvec{v} = 17 x^6 + 8 x^5 + 2 x^2 - 2 x + 14 </m>

	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		The polynomials involved are all degree <m> 6 </m>,
		so assume we are working in <m> \poly_6(\R) </m>.
		Create a matrix <m> A </m> whose columns are the coordinate vectors of the vectors in <m> S </m>,
		relative to the <q>reverse</q> standard basis for <m> \poly_6(\R) </m>:
		<md>
			A =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrrrr}
				1 \amp  1 \amp  1 \amp  2 \amp  1 \\
				1 \amp  1 \amp -1 \amp  2 \amp -2 \\
				0 \amp  0 \amp -1 \amp  0 \amp  0 \\
				0 \amp  0 \amp  3 \amp -1 \amp  2 \\
				0 \amp  1 \amp  0 \amp  1 \amp -3 \\
				0 \amp -1 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp  1 \\
				0 \amp  1 \amp  3 \amp  1 \amp  1
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>.
		Then our question becomes whether the coordinate vector for <m> \uvec{v} </m> is in the column space of <m> A </m>,
		which happens precisely when the system <m> A \uvec{x} = \rmatrixOf{\uvec{v}}{S} </m> is consistent.
		<md>
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrrrr|r}
				1 \amp  1 \amp  1 \amp  2 \amp  1 \amp 17 \\
				1 \amp  1 \amp -1 \amp  2 \amp -2 \amp  8 \\
				0 \amp  0 \amp -1 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp  0 \\
				0 \amp  0 \amp  3 \amp -1 \amp  2 \amp  0 \\
				0 \amp  1 \amp  0 \amp  1 \amp -3 \amp  2 \\
				0 \amp -1 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp  1 \amp -2 \\
				0 \amp  1 \amp  3 \amp  1 \amp  1 \amp 14
			\end{abmatrix}
			\;\; \rowredarrow \;\;
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrrrr|r}
				1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -3 \\
				0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp  5 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp  6 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp  3 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp  0
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>
		Since the system is consistent,
		<m> \uvec{v} </m> is in <m> \Span S </m>,
		with
		<md>\begin{split}
			\uvec{v}
			= (-3) (x^6 + x^5)
			+   5  (x^6 + x^5 + x^2 - x + 1)
			+   0  (x^6 - x^5 - x^4 + 3 x^3 + 3) \\
			+   6  (2 x^6 + 2 x^5 - x^3 + x^2 + 1)
			+   3  (x^6 - 2 x^5 + 2 x^3 - 3 x^2 + x + 1)
			\text{.}
		\end{split}</md>
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
